  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 PM EST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nCurrent-Tonight... Cloud cover blankets much of the southeast U.S. \nthis afternoon, with overcast conditions persisting near and north \nof the I-4 corridor as low pressure continues to lift away from \nthe local area. Sprinkles have been observed beneath low clouds, \nand high-res guidance may suggest areas of sprinkles continuing \nthrough this evening and into early Monday. However, little to no \nrainfall accumulations are expected from this activity. A \ntemperature gradient extends north (L70s) to south (L80s) across \nthe peninsula, following breaks in the cloud cover. West winds \ngusting near 20 mph will subside through this evening as flow \nbegins to lightly shift north-northwest tonight. Low temperatures \nfall to range the upper 50s to low 60s across much of east central\nFlorida early Monday morning. \n\nMonday-Wednesday... A mid-level trough over the central U.S. becomes \nreinforced midweek as a closed low develops over the midwest \nregion. Pulses of shortwave energy pass aloft Tuesday and Wednesday \nas deep troughing approaches the southeast U.S. and northern Gulf. A \nweak area of high pressure will influence the surface pattern on \nMonday. Low pressure begins to organize in the southeastern Gulf \nlate Tuesday, tracking near or south of the area and into the \nwestern Atlantic on Wednesday. Despite increased cloudiness through \nthe period, mostly dry conditions are expected Monday and Tuesday. \nHowever, sprinkles or virga will still be possible at times, \nespecially on Tuesday. Chances for measurable rainfall return \nTuesday night and on Wednesday with greatest chances (60-80%) \nforecast along the Treasure Coast. Elsewhere, PoPs widely range \n30-50%. Latest ensemble guidance keeps mean precip accumulations \naround 0.5\" over areas of Volusia and Brevard that continue to \nrecover from significant floods. The highest precip accumulations \nare highlighted over the south, closest to the passing surface \nlow, and ensemble means may suggest more widespread totals over 1\"\nfor portions of the Treasure Coast. That being said, if the low \nwere to track more north, precipitation means could wiggle a \nlittle higher across the area. Temperatures are forecast near or \nslightly below seasonal values through the period, mostly \nspreading the 70s.\n\nHigh astronomical tides (king tides) are forecast this week. Tidal \nguidance may suggest morning high tide cycles approaching near \nCoastal Flood Advisory thresholds Tuesday and Wednesday mornings. At \nleast a Moderate Risk of rip currents will be present at area \nbeaches. Heed the advice of lifeguards, beach patrol flags, and \nsigns. \n\nThursday-Saturday... Low pressure lifts north-northeast along the \neastern U.S. seaboard Thursday and Friday. Modest rain chances may \nlinger early Thursday before drier air builds with the next frontal \npassage. The current forecast keeps rain chances less than 20% \nFriday and Saturday, but a wide spread of ensemble solutions may \nintroduce more uncertainty in any precip chances by the weekend. \nTemperatures will widely range the upper 60s (north) to upper 70s \n(south) on Thursday as a cooler airmass advects behind the front. \nBelow normal temperatures are then forecast Friday and Saturday as \nhighs spread the mid 60s to mid 70s and low temperatures range the \n50s. Lingering high astronomical tides through the end of the week \nmay continue to produce minor coastal flooding during morning high \ntide cycles.",
